%[text] %[text:anchor:T_1948] # Algorithms
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:../../MainMenu.m)
%[text] The formal name for a clearly defined process that could be followed by a computer is an algorithm. You already know several algorithms from mathematics, and you may have encountered them elsewhere in your life as well for processes like searching for a word in a paper dictionary or sorting a hand of cards into a standard order. You may be less familiar with expressing algorithms themselves clearly and concisely.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A flowchart presentation of the sieve of Eratosthenes, as presented in section "Coding a Simple Math Problem"](text:image:4b5f)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8e9d] ## Communicating Algorithms
%[text] There are many ways to express an algorithm, but the most common are to write out the procedure in standard sentences or in pseudocode. Pseudocode is a term that means a small collection of formal words and symbols that can be combined with formatting and small amounts of text to describe an algorithm that could be implemented in any programming language by a programmer who is familiar with the language.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_09dc] ### Common features of pseudocode
%[text] 1. A symbol that denotes that the rest of the line is a comment. Coming from a MATLAB environment, this script will use `%` as the comment character.
%[text] 2. `a` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `b` means "a is assigned the value of b"
%[text] 3. Standard mathematical symbols such as +, \*, \< as well as mathematical symbols from discrete mathematics such as \\ ("set difference"), ~ ("logical not"), & ("logical and"), and == ("logical equals").
%[text] 4. Sequence identification `startPoint to endPoint` means the set of value \
%[text]{"align":"center"} `{StartPoint, StartPoint + 1, StartPoint + 2, ..., EndPoint - 2, EndPoint -1, EndPoint`}`.` 
%[text]             Similarly, if the step size between values isn't 1, that can be indicated by 
%[text]{"align":"center"} `StartPoint by StepSize to EndPoint` 
%[text]             to mean the list of values 
%[text]{"align":"center"} `{StartPoint, StartPoint + StepSize, StartPoint + 2*StepSize, ... , StartPoint+m*StepSize}` 
%[text]             where (`EndPoint-StartPoint)/(m+1) < StepSize, but (EndPointSstartPoint)/m` $\\geq${"altText":"greater than or equal to"} S`tepSize.`
%[text]           5\. Standard control logic: `while, for, if/else.` 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_07fc] ### Example: Expressing the Simple Square Chaos Game as an Algorithm
%[text] `StartingPoint` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `random point`
%[text] `NumPts` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `number of iterations`
%[text] `for k = 1 to NumPts`
%[text]  `NewCorner` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `randomly selected corner coordinates`
%[text]  `NextPoint` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `StartingPoint + (NewCorner-StartingPoint)/2`
%[text]  `Graph NextPoint`
%[text]  `StartingPoint` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} N`extPoint`
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9589] ## Coding a Simple Math Problem
%[text] What are all the primes less than or equal to 100? This is a computationally straight-forward problem with several common algorithmic solutions. A positive integer $n${"altText":"n"} is prime if its only factors are $1${"altText":"1"} and $n${"altText":"n"}. 
%[text] **Algorithm 1:** Iteratively build the list of all primes less than or equal to $n${"altText":"n"} up to `MaximumValue`
%[text]  `% Recognize that 2 is the smallest prime.` 
%[text]  `% When n=2, the only prime less than or equal to n is` $2${"altText":"2"}`.`
%[text]  `PrimeList` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `2`
%[text]  `for n in 3 to MaximumValue`
%[text]  `for p in PrimeList`
%[text]  `if n` [`mod`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.mod.html) `p == 0`
%[text]  `n is composite and can be ignored`
%[text]  `else if no primes in PrimeList are factors of n`
%[text]  `append n to PrimeList`
PrimeList = 2;
for n = 3:100
    NotPrimeFlag = 0;
    for p = PrimeList
        if mod(n,p) == 0
            NotPrimeFlag = 1;
        elseif p == PrimeList(end) && NotPrimeFlag == 0
            PrimeList = [PrimeList n];
        end
    end
end
PrimeList
%%
%[text] **Algorithm 2:** Assume all numbers are possible up to `maximumValue`, then iteratively eliminate multiples of smaller values.
%[text]  `primeList` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `2 to maximumValue`
%[text]  `p` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `2`
%[text]  `index` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `1`
%[text]  `while p < sqrt(maximumValue)`
%[text]  `primeList` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `primeList \ (2*p by p to maximumValue)`
%[text]  `index` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `index + 1`
%[text]  `p` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `primeList(index)`
%[text] ![](text:image:2d18) **Exercise 1**.
%[text]     a) Implement Algorithm 2. 
%[text]     b) Time how long Algorithm 1 and Algorithm 2 take to determine all the primes up to 100. Which implementation is more efficient? Why?
%[text]     c) Time how long Algorithm 1 and Algorithm 2 take to determine all the primes up to 10,000. Which implementation is more efficient? Why?
%[text] ![](text:image:9df8) **Pro-tip**. Remember that you can use the documentation to search for functions, optional arguments, and syntax. Try a couple of options here such as the general web search:
%[text] ```
%[text] set operations MATLAB
%[text] ```
%[text] Or a specific local documentation search such as:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] doc set difference
%[text] ```
%[text] The challenge is if you don't remember standard terminology, in which case searching may be awkward or impossible until you can determine what other people call whatever you are looking for.
% % Solution to Exercise 1
tic
pMax = 100;
primeList = 2:pMax;
p = 2;
index = 1;
while p < sqrt(pMax)
    primeList = setdiff(primeList,2*p:p:pMax);
    index = index + 1;
    p = primeList(index);
end
toc
primeList
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_106b] ### Testing and Proof of Correctness
%[text] While the unavoidable test of correctness for code is whether it compiles and runs, it is also important to ensure that it correctly implements the algorithm, and that the algorithm solves the problem it is intended to solve. In fact, there is an error in either Algorithm 1 or Algorithm 2. 
%[text] ![](text:image:33f0) **Exercise 2**.
%[text] Use both Algorithm 1 and Algorithm 2 to compute the primes less than or equal to 25. Do you get the same result? If not, what is the difference and why? How do you fix the appropriate algorithm? What does that do to your code?
%[text] **Exercise 2 Solution**
%[text] No, Algorithm 1 correctly computes the primes but Algorithm 2 claims that 5 and 25 are prime numbers because the while loop does not execute for p = sqrt(25) = 5. To fix the algorithm, you need to modify the conditional to be while `p <= sqrt(maximumValue)` rather than a strict inequality. This is a minor change in the code.
% % Corrected Algorithm 2 implementation
tic
pMax = 25;
primeList = 2:pMax;
p = 2;
index = 1;
while p <= sqrt(pMax)
    primeList = setdiff(primeList,2*p:p:pMax);
    index = index + 1;
    p = primeList(index);
end
toc
primeList
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4497] #### Unit Testing
%[text] By breaking down your code into simple components where you can check if the input and outputs match what you expect, you can formally or informally check that your code behaves correctly. This can also help in isolating the cause of a bug that you know about but haven't determined a fix for yet. If we consider the implementation of Algorithm 1, then the smallest units would be individual lines such as
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] primeList = [primeList n];
%[text] ```
%[text] or
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] primeList = setdiff(primeList,2*p:p:pMax);
%[text] ```
%[text] and running the unit test could be simply running the code with that line unsuppressed, or if you don't have the whole program built yet you can run the unit test by defining the values that will come in and running the unit test to see what comes out. 
% Set values for testing
PrimeList = [2 3 5 7];
n = 11;

% Expected Result?
PrimeListExpected = [2 3 5 7 11]

% Run test
PrimeList = [PrimeList n]
%[text] For larger programs or outputs, you might not want to check by hand. In general, high level programming languages have built-in functions to help with testing such as the ability to throw an exception by responding to particular types of errors with a standard message or by documenting assumptions within your code with assertions. 
assert(isequal(PrimeList,PrimeListExpected))
%[text] ![](text:image:0770) **Try**. Modify the code above so that `primeList` does not match `primeListExpected` and run the code again. What happens now?
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C69F7A89] In the case of a branch or a loop, you want to test that the logic follows the path you were planning by setting up input values leading to each path through the code. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7bca] #### Edge Cases
%[text] One common class of programming errors are those that arise along boundaries. Did you check what happens at very large or very small values? What about 0, which is the boundary between positive and negative numbers? Are there any inequalities? Should they be strict or not? 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_62a7] ### ![](text:image:51ad)**Application: Caesar Cipher**
%[text] [`PleaseCodeThis(InputString,InShift)`](internal:M_5154) is a function that takes a string and a shift and implements the Caesar cipher which shifts each letter of the alphabet forward (or backward) by `InShift` places. 
function OutString = PleaseCodeThis(InputString,InShift) %[text:anchor:M_5154]
% Convert the InputString to ASCII
NumString = int8(char(InputString));
OutString = NumString;
BdryVals = int8('azAZ');
% Check that the input character is a letter and if so, encode it
% appropriately
for k = 1:length(NumString)
    if (NumString(k) >= BdryVals(1) && NumString(k) <= BdryVals(2))||(NumString(k) >= BdryVals(3) && NumString(k) <= BdryVals(4))
        OutString(k) = NumString(k)+InShift;
    end
end
% Convert the ASCII back to a string
OutString = convertCharsToStrings(char(OutString));
end
%[text] Historically, this was commonly implemented with nested disks. In the model below, the outer ring is fixed and the inner ring can spin. 
%[text] ![](text:image:43f4) **Try**. Run the code below with different arguments by changing the value of the slider. 
shift = -18; %[control:slider:5a07]{"position":[9,12]}
CaesarCipherTool(shift) %[output:2c7c6c56]
%[text] 
PleaseCodeThis("Once upon a time...",shift) %[output:3d3c9352]
%[text] ![](text:image:6be6) **Exercise 3**.
%[text] The function [`PleaseCodeThis`](internal:M_5154) only works in some cases. For what values does it fail and why? Explain how you would fix it. 
%[text] **Exercise 3 Solution**
%[text] The function [`PleaseCodeThis`](internal:M_5154) only works when the shift argument is less than the distance from any of the characters in the string to the end of the alphabet. The problem is illustrated above with the cipher wheel. The cipher is supposed to simply cycle from z to a (or Z to A) as necessary, but the code currently just adds the shift without checking it if results in a value requiring looping to the beginning of the alphabet. To fix the code, you would need to implement some modular arithmetic checks. 
%[text] Example Caesar cipher encoder implemented correctly
% Example function with cyclic shifting implemented correctly
function outString = CorrectedPleaseCodeThis(inString,inShift)
% Convert the InputString to ASCII
numString = int8(char(inString));
outString = zeros(size(numString));
bdryVals = int8('azAZ');
% Check that the input character is an English letter and if so, encode it
% appropriately, if not, just leave it
for k = 1:length(numString)
    if numString(k)>=bdryVals(1) && numString(k)<=bdryVals(2)
        outString(k) = mod(numString(k)-bdryVals(1)+inShift,26)+bdryVals(1);
    elseif numString(k)>=bdryVals(3) && numString(k)<=bdryVals(4)
        outString(k) = mod(numString(k)-bdryVals(3)+inShift,26)+bdryVals(3);
    else
        outString(k) = numString(k);
    end
end
% Convert the ASCII back to a string
outString = convertCharsToStrings(char(outString));
end
%[text] Now, testing the new implementation we have:
CorrectedPleaseCodeThis("The quick brown fox jumped over the lazy dog.",shift) %[output:8e0bf384]
