%[text] ![](text:image:4736) **Exercise 4**.
%[text] Implement a simple visual cryptography algorithm that transforms a black and white image into two chaotic-appearing images that are in fact carefully balanced compositions with equal numbers of white pixels and black pixels. Briefly, replace each white pixel with four new pixels in each cryptographically transformed image-pair:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A square in four quadrants, clockwise from the top left: black, white, black, white](text:image:4cab)   and   ![A square in four quadrants, clockwise from the top left: black, white, black, white](text:image:258a)                or                  ![A square in four quadrants, clockwise from the top left: white, black, white, black](text:image:0fa0)   and   ![A square in four quadrants, clockwise from the top left: white, black, white, black](text:image:1ab6),
%[text] and replace each black pixel in the original image similarly with either
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A square in four quadrants, clockwise from the top left: black, white, black, white](text:image:1893)   and   ![A square in four quadrants, clockwise from the top left: white, black, white, black](text:image:0a5f)                or                  ![A square in four quadrants, clockwise from the top left: white, black, white, black](text:image:6809)   and   ![A square in four quadrants, clockwise from the top left: black, white, black, white](text:image:41cd).
%[text] When recombined, tracking only the black pixels, note that the white regions are now exactly 50% white pixels and 50% black pixels in random combinations that will appear to the eye as grey at sufficient scale. The black regions are preserved. This also makes it possible to rescale and recolor back to the original image with complete fidelity if desired.
%[text] **Simple Visual Cryptography Algorithm**
%[text] `StartingImage` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `matrix of 1's and 0's`
%[text] `sx` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `number of rows in StartingImage`
%[text] `sy` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `number of columns in StartingImage`
%[text] `Initialize two matrices, im1 and im2 of size 2*sx by 2*sy`
%[text] `for j = 1 to sx`
%[text]  `for k = 1 to sy`
%[text]  `flip = random choice of 0 or 1`
%[text]  `if StartingImage(j,k) == 1` 
%[text]  `if flip == 0`
%[text]  `im1(2*j-1:2*j,2*k-1:2*k)` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `[1 0;0 1]`
%[text]  `im2(2*j-1:2*j,2*k-1:2*k)` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `[0 1;1 0]`
%[text]  `else`
%[text]   `im2(2*j-1:2*j,2*k-1:2*k)` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `[1 0;0 1]`
%[text]  `im1(2*j-1:2*j,2*k-1:2*k)` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `[0 1;1 0]`
%[text]  `else`
%[text]  `if flip == 0`
%[text]  `im1(2*j-1:2*j,2*k-1:2*k)` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `[1 0;0 1]`
%[text]  `im2(2*j-1:2*j,2*k-1:2*k)` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `[1 0;0 1]`
%[text]  `else`
%[text]   `im2(2*j-1:2*j,2*k-1:2*k)` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `[0 1;1 0]`
%[text]  `im1(2*j-1:2*j,2*k-1:2*k)` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `[0 1;1 0]` 
%[text] Implement this algorithm. Test your results on the following images you have already played with:
% Clear the workspace of all current variables
clearvars()
% Load sample images
load("BW_Images.mat")
% Print a list of images that are now in the workspace
whos
%%
% Select an image and call it im0

im0 = mouse;

% Code solution to Exercise 4 here to transform im0 into two images im1 and
% im2 using the visual cryptography algorithm given
[sx,sy] = size(im0);
im1 = zeros(2*[sx,sy]);
im2 = im1;
for k = 1:sx
    for j = 1:sy
        flip = randi(2,1);
        if im0(k,j) == 1 && flip == 2
            im1(2*k-1:2*k,2*j-1:2*j) = [1 0; 0 1];
            im2(2*k-1:2*k,2*j-1:2*j) = [0 1; 1 0];
        elseif im0(k,j) == 1 && flip == 1
            im2(2*k-1:2*k,2*j-1:2*j) = [1 0; 0 1];
            im1(2*k-1:2*k,2*j-1:2*j) = [0 1; 1 0];
        elseif im0(k,j) == 0 && flip == 2
            im1(2*k-1:2*k,2*j-1:2*j) = [1 0;0 1];
            im2(2*k-1:2*k,2*j-1:2*j) = [1 0;0 1];
        else
            im1(2*k-1:2*k,2*j-1:2*j) = [0 1;1 0];
            im2(2*k-1:2*k,2*j-1:2*j) = [0 1;1 0];
        end
    end
end
montage({im0,abs(im1-im2),im1,im2})

